    "question": {
      ":question/id": "53eaef9e-ac84-5e28-86a3-fbb695aa212c",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $K(x,y,z)=\\langle 4x, x^2, 1\\rangle$\n- **II.** $A(t)=\\langle 4t, t^2, 1\\rangle$\n- **III.** $X(x,y,z)=\\langle 4x, x^2, 1\\rangle$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $A(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- The function $X(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
