  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186172087,
      "id": "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb",
      "title": "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1ad38f73-d09f-57c6-9759-5e95bccd5454",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $X(x,y,z)=\\langle 7x, x^2, 1\\rangle$\n- **II.** $A(t)=\\langle 7t, t^2, 1\\rangle$\n- **III.** $Y(x,y)=\\langle 7x, x^2, 1\\rangle$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $X(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $A(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- The function $Y(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "137c5beb-7f09-5b5b-8e0c-3d44d912fec4",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
