      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$\n- **II.** $D(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$\n- **III.** $Q(t)=\\langle 2t, t^2, 1\\rangle$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $D(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $Q(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
